@page "/components/menus"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Menus

<DocsHeader Title="Tabs, dropdowns and tooltips" Eyebrow="Components">
    <Lead>Tabs to switch between panels, dropdown menus for actions, and tooltips for short hints.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<h2 id="tabs">Tabs</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Tabs)">
    <p>
        <code>StilTabs</code> follows the WAI-ARIA tabs pattern. Only the selected tab is in the tab order; the arrow keys,
        Home and End move between tabs and select them, and disabled tabs are skipped. Name the tab list with
        <code>Label</code>. Bind the selected tab with <code>@@bind-ActiveIndex</code>. Switching tabs needs an interactive
        render mode. For navigation between pages, use a <a href="components/navigation#nav">nav</a> instead.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="pills">Pills, ids and kept panels</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(PillTabs)">
    <p>
        <code>Appearance="StilTabsAppearance.Pills"</code> fills the selected tab. Give each tab an <code>Id</code> and bind
        <code>@@bind-ActiveId</code> when tabs can be added or removed. Only the selected panel is rendered, unless
        <code>KeepAlive</code> is set: then every panel stays rendered and hidden, so form input survives switching.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="dropdowns">Dropdowns</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Dropdowns)">
    <p>
        <code>StilDropdown</code> is a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> element, so it opens without interactivity.
        <code>stil.js</code> closes it on an outside click, on Escape and after choosing an item, and the arrow keys move
        between items. Items are buttons (<code>OnClick</code>) or links (<code>Href</code>); group them with
        <code>StilDropdownHeader</code> and <code>StilDropdownDivider</code>. For an icon-only toggle, use
        <code>Toggle</code>, <code>IconOnly</code> and an <code>AriaLabel</code>. <code>AlignEnd</code> aligns the menu
        with the end of the toggle and <code>DropUp</code> opens it above.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="menu-data">Menus from data</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(MenuFromData)">
    <p>
        Build the menu from <code>StilMenuItem</code> values: <code>Action</code>, <code>Link</code>, <code>Header</code> and
        <code>Divider</code>. <code>@@bind-Open</code> follows whether the menu is open.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="tooltips">Tooltips</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(Tooltips)">
    <p>
        <code>StilTooltip</code> shows <code>Text</code> above its content on hover and keyboard focus, or below with
        <code>Position="StilTooltipPosition.Bottom"</code>. It is CSS only and works in every render mode. Screen readers do
        not read the tooltip, so the wrapped element needs its own accessible name, such as its text or an
        <code>aria-label</code>. Do not put essential information only in a tooltip.
    </p>
</DocExample>

